      "id": 1,
      "text": "Erskine was founded in 1889 as a station stop on the Great Northern Railway and named after banker George Quincy Erskine.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erskine%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Passenger trains on the Soo Line served Erskine until 1961, and a diamond crossing with an interlocking tower operated there until the early 1960s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/erskine_minnesota",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original Great Northern tracks still run through Erskine today under BNSF ownership, and the historic depot has been repurposed as a maintenance field office.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/erskine_minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Every early June, residents celebrate the Erskine Water Carnival, a local festival that has become a community tradition.",
